{#
 # Cards side by side: one across, then two from md, then three from xl.
 #
 # This is the one grid in the app worth naming. Everywhere else the columns are one-off geometry —
 # 23 of the 35 distinct `col-*` combinations in `templates/` are used exactly once, and a
 # `<twig:Grid:Col cols="{ base: 12, xl: 9 }">` would only be `col-12 col-xl-9` with more syntax.
 # What is written here is a decision instead: how many cards fit before the eye loses the row.
 #
 # It uses CSS Grid rather than Bootstrap's, so the children are the cells — no `.col` wrapper per
 # card — and they stretch to the tallest of their row on their own. That is also what makes the
 # component framework-agnostic in fact and not just in its prop names: nothing about it is
 # Bootstrap's to take away.
 #
 # A column count would be a prop the day a third call site wants a different one. Two sites want
 # the same, so it is not one yet.
 #}
<div{{ attributes.defaults({ class: 'app-card-grid' }) }}>
    {% block content %}{% endblock %}
</div>
